          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Broadly, by
            %em deformation quantization
            one means notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big(X, \{-,-\}\big)
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/families"} families
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebra"} non-commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras"} algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\hbar}
            parameterized by specific admissible (formal) values of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            including
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0 = C^\infty(X)
            is the ordinary commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\hbar}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
            at that value of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_0
            in a way that increases with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            and is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal"} infinitesimally
            controlled by the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
          %p Deformation quantization is often taken by default to refer to the historically first notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %strong>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
              ,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            is just a formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variable"} variable
            (i.e. an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal"} infinitesimal
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number"} number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            of all algebras in question is that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
          %p
            One might naively imagine that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            appearing in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            have a finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \in \mathbb{R}_+
            thus yielding actual (non-formal) deformation quantizations for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar \lt \epsilon
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "C*+algebraic+deformation+quantization#ConvergingFormalDeformationQuantization"} here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            makes manifest that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
            conditions typically force admissible values of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            to form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace"} subspace
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_+
            with only an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accumulation+point"} accumulation point
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar = 0
            (namely, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fraction"} inverse
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/\hbar
            is typically constrained to come in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} integral
            multiples).
          %p Therefore, beyond formal deformation quantization there are notions of
          %ul
            %li
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantizations"} strict deformation quantizations
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            is allowed to take discrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real values
            with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accumulation+point"} accumulation point
            at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar = 0
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} of observables
          %p
            In its focus on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            the notion of deformation quantization is roughly dual to
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
